{
  "dom_id": "step-e9403",
  "name": "Example: Subtracting a Positive Fraction From a Fraction",
  "problem": "Find the value of $\\dfrac{5}{12} - \\dfrac{11}{12}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Since both fractions have the same denominators, we can combine them into one fraction, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{12} - \\dfrac{11}{12} = \\dfrac{5 - 11}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $5 - 11$ using a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14148354/assets/d9367da101187cf8861458e58f16ea179cff66477d1b9060d3ae199ff8bb3843.png)\n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 - 11}{12} = \\dfrac{-6}{12} = -\\dfrac{6}{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{6}{12} = -\\dfrac{6 \\div 6}{12 \\div 6} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{12} - \\dfrac{11}{12} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
